<ArticleLayout slug="buying-metalware-from-moradabad-without-getting-burned">
  <p>
    Moradabad has made metal for four hundred years and supplies a very large share of the world's
    hotel metalware. It is also a city where it is entirely possible to place an order with a company
    that owns no machinery, receive a sample made by one workshop and a shipment made by another, and
    have no recourse when the two do not match.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are a factory here, so treat this as interested advice. It is still accurate.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Establish whether you are talking to a factory</h2>
  <p>
    Perhaps four in five companies presenting themselves as manufacturers in this city are trading
    houses. That is not automatically bad — a good trader with a stable of workshops can serve you
    well. It matters because it determines who can answer a question and who can fix a problem.
  </p>
  <p>Three questions that settle it in about ninety seconds:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>"Which operations happen in your building?"</strong> A factory answers with a list. A trader answers with an adjective.</li>
    <li><strong>"Can we video-call from the polishing bay right now?"</strong> Not a scheduled tour — right now, unannounced. This is the single most effective test available to a remote buyer, and it costs nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>"Who cuts your dies?"</strong> If tooling is outsourced, every bespoke piece is at the mercy of a third party's schedule, and your pattern is sitting in somebody else's tool room.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>2. The three substitutions to check for</h2>
  <p>Each of these is invisible on arrival and expensive eighteen months later.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Steel grade.</strong> 201 substituted for 304. About a fifth cheaper, indistinguishable by eye, and it pits in a coastal or chlorinated environment. A magnet is a crude test; a mill test certificate is the real one, and you are entitled to ask for it.</li>
    <li><strong>Sprayed "antiquing".</strong> Chemical antiquing goes into the metal. Sprayed antiquing sits on it and comes off. If every piece in a large batch is identical, it was sprayed.</li>
    <li><strong>Unlined copper and brass.</strong> Covered above — a food-safety failure, not a cost saving.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Gauge is where quotes are really won</h2>
  <p>
    When two quotes for the same item differ by thirty percent, the difference is usually metal
    thickness rather than skill or margin. A thinner-gauge tray looks identical in a photograph, feels
    wrong in the hand, and develops a wavy rim after a season of stacking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Specify gauge or weight in your enquiry.</strong> Asking for "a 14-inch hammered brass
    tray, minimum 1.0 kg" makes every quote you receive comparable and makes it obvious who was
    planning to under-build. It is the single most useful line you can add to an RFQ.
  </p>

  <h2>4. What a good supplier will let you do before you pay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Walk the floor on an unscheduled video call.</li>
    <li>Send a paid sample, with the cost credited against your first production order.</li>
    <li>Appoint your own inspection agency — SGS, Bureau Veritas, Intertek or your own agent — at no obstruction and no charge for access.</li>
    <li>Receive photographs of the packed batch before the container is sealed.</li>
    <li>Hold tooling you paid for exclusively to you, in writing.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Refusal on any of these is information. Refusal on the inspection point is decisive.</p>

  <h2>5. Structure the first order so a mistake is survivable</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Sample first, always.</strong> Even at 7–10 days, it is the cheapest insurance available.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep the first order small enough to lose.</strong> One item, one finish, a quantity you could write off without a difficult conversation.</li>
    <li><strong>Use FOB on a first order.</strong> It keeps the freight decision with your own forwarder, who will usually beat a manufacturer's rate, and it limits what can go wrong to the part the manufacturer controls.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay 30% advance against a proforma invoice, balance against a copy B/L.</strong> This is the normal Indian export term. A supplier demanding 100% up front on a first order is asking you to carry all of the risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Put the specification in writing, in the PI.</strong> Grade, gauge, finish, dimensions, packing. A specification that lives only in a WhatsApp thread is not a specification.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>6. The commercial questions worth asking early</h2>
  <p>
    Ask about MOQ per item rather than per order — a good supplier will let you mix items across one
    order. Ask what happens to your tooling. Ask whether they will consolidate a container with your
    other Moradabad suppliers, which usually saves more than it costs. And ask what their spares
    position looks like in three years, because a chafing dish with no available hinge pin is a
    disposable chafing dish.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    Verify the factory with an unscheduled call. Specify grade and gauge in writing. Sample first,
    inspect before shipment, and keep the first order small. Do those five things and Moradabad is one
    of the best value propositions in the world for hospitality metalware. Skip them and it is a
    lottery.
  </p>
  <p>
    If it is useful, we will answer any of these questions about our own operation directly —
    <a href="/contact">including the video call</a>, unannounced, whenever suits you.
  </p>
